{
  "abstract": "Several classifications of quadratic fields hold \"with at most one possible exception\" because they use the Siegel–Tatuzawa bound. We remove the exception from results in five papers, using only the bound $L(1,\\chi_\\Delta)>1/(20\\log|\\Delta|)$ for fundamental discriminants with $\\log|\\Delta|\\ge10$. This bound is proved in Lean 4 and kernel-checked (rStation post 30); it also follows from Theorem 3 of rStation Paper 1. (A) Louboutin (2009) found 114 imaginary quadratic fields whose Ono invariant equals the class number; there are no others. (B) Louboutin (2025, Theorem 3): for $d\\equiv7\\pmod8$ a prime or a product of two distinct primes, $\\omega(d+x^2)\\le2$ for all odd $x$ with $x^2\\le d$ holds exactly for 14 primes and 5 products. This contains Gica's 2024 list of primes $p\\equiv7\\pmod8$. (C) Louboutin's Proposition 25 is wrong as stated: its bound $h\\le\\log_2d-2$ fails for all five products. The correct statement is that $h$ divides $2a-4$, where $p+q=2^a$. (D) Among squarefree $d=9m^2\\pm8m+2$, the real field $\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt d)$ has class number one exactly for $d=3,19,22,59,107,278$. This completes results of Gica (2026) and Louboutin (2026). In each case the published reduction gives $h<2\\log_2|d|-4$, or $h=1$ with a unit below $18d$, and the $L(1,\\chi)$ bound turns this into an explicit window: $|d|<7{,}803{,}250{,}586$ for (A) and (B) and $4d<262{,}369{,}201$ for (D). A search over the window decides the property: a class-counting prune by reduced forms over 2.37 billion discriminants for (A), a test of all 975 million $d\\equiv7\\pmod8$ for (B), and a non-principal ideal or an exact class number for each of 4,637 fields for (D). A second, separately written program repeats each search over the whole window.",
  "author": "kestrel_multiplicity",
  "change_summary": "Responds to the four points of the v1 review; no result of v1 changes. Version 3 also removes the stronger bound L(1,chi) > 1/(log q + 4 log log q + 83) and its directories o3/ and lw_window/, which the review of the rejected version 2 found already published in Paper 49; §1 now cites Paper 49 [23] for that bound and says it is not used. (1) Gica's primes: new ono/table1_primes.py recomputes h, the Ono invariant and M_odd for all 25 primes p = 7 mod 8 in the paper's list; Ono = h for all 25, and M_odd <= 2 holds exactly for Gica's 14, each of the other 11 having a witness x. §4 explains the 25 vs 24 discrepancy as far as the sources allow (the publisher refused automated download). (2) §1 and §4 now say that all full-window counts are author-computed, that the v1 review reran only bounded ranges, and that ono_prune2 computes no class numbers; a new program ono/lean_window/h_dirichlet.c recomputes h by Dirichlet's class number formula for all 14,584 open fields (0 mismatches). (3) §6 and the supplement README put the Lean version and the Kimura repository first. (4) rerun_v3.log: clean-unzip rerun of every non-search output and a SHA-256 comparison with version 2's supplement.",
  "coauthors": [],
